  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 218 AM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nCurrent Conditions/Today:\n\nSurface high pressure will be meandering around Lake Superior today \nwith northeasterly winds off of the Lake pushing marine fog inland \nthis morning. This fog is expected to retreat back to the Lake by \nlate morning. Largely quiet conditions for the day with temperatures \nwarming into the 80s. Not quite as muggy as yesterday away from the \nlake with dewpoints staying out of the 70s. Can't fully rule out \nsome isolated showers and storms as a weak surface low moves through \nOntario this afternoon. This could lead to some surface convergence \nalong the return flow of the high pressure over Lake Superior. The \nmost likely area to see any activity would across northern MN and \ninto the Arrowhead, but chances are still fairly low 15-25%. If a \nstorm does manage to develop it will have some instability to work \nwith but the low shear environment should keep things from getting \nto severe criteria. There will also still be plenty of smoke aloft \nin the skies which may increase the CIN further lowering chances of \nanything developing. \n\nTomorrow:\n\nThe warming trend continues with widespread upper 80s and some low \n90s across the region. And with the dewpoints approaching the upper \n60s we will see those heat indicies climb a few degrees higher. \nBroad ridging aloft will be in place helping to suppress any \nwidespread precipitation chances. But some diurnally driven activity \nwill be possible in the afternoon hours which could be slightly \namplified by any ridge runners. However, most the CAMs and the \ndeterministic guidance keeps PoPs at bay through the day. For now, \nwe have limited the chances to less than 20%. \n\nLate Week:\n\nOur next significant weather maker arrives on Thursday and Friday as \na shortwave trough and associated cold front approach from the west. \nThis system will bring our next widespread chance for showers and \nthunderstorms, and possibly some severe threat too though shear \nlooks a little weak at the moment and could be the limiting factor. \nAs southerly flow increases ahead of the front, humidity levels will \ncreep back up, maintaining high temperatures in the upper 80s \ndespite the increased cloud cover and precipitation chances.\n\nWeekend:\n\nLooking ahead to the upcoming weekend, the Northland will remain in \na somewhat stagnant weather pattern. Afternoon highs will generally \nstay in the 80s. With the main storm track positioned to our north, \nwe can expect occasional, less organized waves of showers and storms \nto dive across the area.",
